WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.629
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:18.479
Unit five: Behavioural Economics. In this unit, you will understand how psychology shapes our economic decisions.

4
00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:20.333
nudge.

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:26.342
nudge means a small change that gently encourages people to choose something.

6
00:00:26.642 --> 00:00:30.487
Placing fruit at eye level is a nudge towards healthier eating.

7
00:00:31.187 --> 00:00:34.188
Your turn. Say: nudge.

8
00:00:36.888 --> 00:00:37.587
nudge.

9
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:40.026
cognitive bias.

10
00:00:40.726 --> 00:00:45.825
cognitive bias means a common error in thinking that affects our judgements.

11
00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:49.912
Confirmation bias is a well-known cognitive bias.

12
00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:53.961
Your turn. Say: cognitive bias.

13
00:00:56.661 --> 00:00:58.173
cognitive bias.

14
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:00.486
loss aversion.

15
00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:06.808
loss aversion means the tendency to fear losses more than we value equal gains.

16
00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:10.872
Loss aversion explains why people keep losing investments.

17
00:01:11.572 --> 00:01:15.374
Your turn. Say: loss aversion.

18
00:01:18.074 --> 00:01:19.284
loss aversion.

19
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.215
anchoring.

20
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.633
anchoring means relying too much on the first number or fact you hear.

21
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:29.314
Shops use anchoring by showing a high 'original' price.

22
00:01:30.014 --> 00:01:32.585
Your turn. Say: anchoring.

23
00:01:35.285 --> 00:01:36.193
anchoring.

24
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:38.347
herd behaviour.

25
00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:43.752
herd behaviour means following what a crowd does instead of deciding independently.

26
00:01:44.052 --> 00:01:47.096
Herd behaviour can cause sudden market crashes.

27
00:01:47.796 --> 00:01:51.041
Your turn. Say: herd behaviour.

28
00:01:53.741 --> 00:01:54.834
herd behaviour.

29
00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.327
rational choice.

30
00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:04.206
rational choice means a decision based purely on logic and self-interest.

31
00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:08.747
Classical economics assumes everyone makes a rational choice.

32
00:02:09.447 --> 00:02:12.761
Your turn. Say: rational choice.

33
00:02:15.461 --> 00:02:16.949
rational choice.

34
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:18.986
incentive.

35
00:02:19.686 --> 00:02:22.788
incentive means something that motivates people to act.

36
00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:27.027
Tax discounts are an incentive to install solar panels.

37
00:02:27.727 --> 00:02:31.145
Your turn. Say: incentive.

38
00:02:33.845 --> 00:02:34.974
incentive.

39
00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:37.344
impulse control.

40
00:02:38.044 --> 00:02:41.936
impulse control means the ability to resist sudden desires.

41
00:02:42.236 --> 00:02:44.956
Online shopping tests our impulse control.

42
00:02:45.656 --> 00:02:48.703
Your turn. Say: impulse control.

43
00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:52.671
impulse control.

44
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:55.927
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.227 --> 00:03:00.421
Rithy, do you think people usually make rational choices with money?

46
00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:06.180
Not really. Faced with a sale, most of us forget all logic.

47
00:03:07.483 --> 00:03:14.315
That's anchoring. Shown a 'before' price of a hundred dollars, a fifty-dollar price feels like a bargain.

48
00:03:15.617 --> 00:03:22.611
And loss aversion is even stronger. I once kept a bad investment for months, hoping to avoid a loss.

49
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:28.554
Very common. Governments now use nudges to help people decide better.

50
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:34.662
Like automatic pension savings? Once enrolled, people rarely opt out.

51
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.994
Exactly. Simple incentives can change behaviour more than long lectures.

52
00:03:42.283 --> 00:03:45.757
Though, if overused, nudges might feel manipulative.

53
00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.837
Grammar focus: Participle and verbless clauses.

54
00:03:51.137 --> 00:03:55.227
Participle and verbless clauses make sentences shorter and more formal.

55
00:03:55.527 --> 00:04:00.420
Faced with a sale, we forget logic. Once enrolled, people rarely leave.

56
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.310
The understood subject must match the subject of the main clause.

57
00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:11.242
These compact clauses make academic writing concise; make sure the subject matches the main clause.

58
00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.912
Now read and listen. Why We Don't Behave Like Economists.

59
00:04:16.212 --> 00:04:20.894
Traditional economics assumed that people always make rational choices.

60
00:04:22.183 --> 00:04:27.840
Behavioural economists, however, have shown that our decisions are full of predictable errors.

61
00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.866
Asked to estimate a price, we are strongly influenced by the first number we hear.

62
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.261
Fearing losses more than we value gains, we often hold on to bad investments.

63
00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.187
Herd behaviour makes us follow crowds, even when they are wrong.

64
00:04:46.483 --> 00:04:52.175
Recognising these biases, governments began designing 'nudges' to guide better choices.

65
00:04:53.483 --> 00:04:59.512
Once introduced, automatic saving plans dramatically increased pension savings in several countries.

66
00:05:00.817 --> 00:05:05.336
Used responsibly, nudges respect freedom while improving wellbeing.

67
00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.256
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:09.556 --> 00:05:12.531
Relying too much on the first number you hear is blank.

69
00:05:12.831 --> 00:05:17.459
A, anchoring. B, herd behaviour. C, incentive.

70
00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:24.622
The answer is A: anchoring.

71
00:05:26.117 --> 00:05:29.695
blank with a difficult choice, she asked for advice.

72
00:05:29.995 --> 00:05:34.206
A, Facing. B, Faced. C, To face.

73
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:40.123
The answer is B: Faced.

74
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:43.686
blank in doubt, check the facts.

75
00:05:43.986 --> 00:05:46.919
A, When. B, Being. C, Had.

76
00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.754
The answer is A: When.

77
00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.620
Fearing losses more than valuing equal gains is blank.

78
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.313
A, loss aversion. B, impulse control. C, rational choice.

79
00:06:08.317 --> 00:06:11.071
The answer is A: loss aversion.

80
00:06:12.583 --> 00:06:16.499
According to the text, what increased pension savings?

81
00:06:16.799 --> 00:06:23.389
A, higher salaries. B, automatic saving plans. C, new taxes.

82
00:06:27.383 --> 00:06:31.182
The answer is B: automatic saving plans.

83
00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:38.575
Now it's your turn. Part one: Are nudges a fair way for governments to influence people?

84
00:06:43.875 --> 00:06:49.694
Part two: State your position, give two reasons, and suggest one nudge for Cambodia.

85
00:06:55.994 --> 00:07:09.272
I think they are fair, if used transparently. Firstly, they preserve freedom of choice; secondly, they are cheap and effective. For instance, Cambodian schools could place water, not sugary drinks, at the front of canteens.

86
00:07:09.572 --> 00:07:12.268
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:13.283 --> 00:07:18.327
Great work today! You learned eight new words and participle and verbless clauses.

88
00:07:18.627 --> 00:07:23.904
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:24.204 --> 00:07:26.902
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:27.202 --> 00:07:29.622
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

